      "id": 1,
      "text": "Greenbush Township was formed in 1868 when the southernmost five survey townships of Alcona County joined together.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenbush%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors a Chippewa chief, Negwegon, whose name means “Little Wing.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://greenbushtwp.net/history/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1910 James Bell bought land on the high banks of Lake Huron, marking a key moment in the town’s agricultural development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://greenbushtwp.net/history/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Greenbush Historical Society Museum, housed in the oldest remaining schoolhouse, opened after the building was donated and renovated by the township.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://museumsdatabase.com/museums/view/15568",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town celebrated its 150th anniversary in 2018 with a parade, craft fair, and a barbecue at the fire station, which itself marked 50 years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenbush%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "A 1968 special edition of the Alcona County Review celebrated Greenbush’s centennial and provided much of the historical record we have today.",
